<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qal'at al-Bahrain</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bahrain Fort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qal'at al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> is an archaeological site on the north coast of Bahrain, occupied from about 2300 BCE to the 18th century. It was a walled town and probably the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a>, and later the site of a fort rebuilt by the Portuguese in the 16th century. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qalat-al-bahrain.jpg" alt="Qal'at al-Bahrain" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Falbisoner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bahrain_Fort_March_2015.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Qal'at al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> stands on the northern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain-island">Bahrain Island</a>, about 6 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manama">Manama</a> on the fertile north coast. It is a tell, an artificial mound about 12 metres high built up by successive layers of human occupation, the largest tell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> region. It was built close to the port and partly on land reclaimed from the shore. The site covers 17.5 hectares and has been called Bahrain's most important site in antiquity. The name means fort of Bahrain, and the fort on top of the mound, Qalʿat al-Burtughal, the Portuguese fort, gave the whole site its name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The layers show a continuous human presence from about 2300 BCE. The earliest settlement on the northern side was a small village, the only one of its period in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, whose people grew crops near the oasis, planted palms, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and goats and fished. Their houses were of rough stone with clay or mortar and had plastered floors, and streets separated the housing blocks.</p><p>Later a walled town grew up, whose fortifications enclosed about 15 hectares and were raised several times, as the gates at four levels show. Streets 12 metres wide ran north to south, and a palace with warehouses stood at the centre. The place prospered until 1800 BCE, after which it was deserted and covered by drifting sand.</p><p>It was once the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> civilisation. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>, Dilmun was the land of immortality, the ancestral place of the Sumerians and a meeting point of gods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> was cast in a large workshop and traded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Finds link the site with Mesopotamia through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscription and hematite, with Oman through steatite bowls, and with the Indus Civilization through carnelian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and a stone weight. Later occupants included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A Danish expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-bibby">Geoffrey Bibby</a> excavated the site from 1954 to 1972, and a French expedition from the National Centre for Scientific Research worked there from the 1970s. About 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets were found, three of them dated to the reign of a Kassite king named Agum. Danish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> archaeologists have dug there since 1987, and about 25 percent of the site has been excavated. It was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2005.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qal'at al-Bahrain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bahrain was governed by Hormuzi governors, while the Portuguese kept a garrison and rebuilt the fort at <b>Qal'at al-Bahrain</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-bahrain">Portuguese Bahrain</a></li></ul></section>
